notes:
Maintainer risk: HIGH — the AddressBook/set onDestroyRemoveContents cascade (P0) and the ContactCard/copy missing-§5.4-mandates set (P0) both produce silent data-integrity violations on the wire that look correct in the test suite, and the README documents the cascade as if it works. A consumer integrating this crate will discover the gaps only by reading the RFC in detail, not by reading the code or running the tests.
Confidence: 12% chance of missed bug.
Least confident flags: JMAP-qz9v.13 (placeholder id dance — might be an established workspace pattern with a doc note I missed) and JMAP-qz9v.17 (register_account empty-string — borderline pedantic for a demo helper).
Uncertainty driven by: did not run any new probes, only reviewed source + existing tests + RFC; did not cross-check every cookie-cutter sibling line-by-line for the same gaps; did not exercise the realistic-demo-ids feature branch.
Would raise confidence if: (a) a probe (filed as JMAP-qz9v.20-style follow-up) that fuzz-tests a cycle of create/destroy/create against MemoryBackend to confirm JMAP-qz9v.14 collision; (b) cross-check pass against calendars-server's /copy path to confirm cookie-cutter divergence is contacts-specific vs workspace-wide; (c) cross-check pass against jmap-jscontact-types to confirm addressBookIds is typed as Map<Id, bool> and the empty-map invariant has no type-level enforcement (assumed but not verified).
API contract risk: MEDIUM — the ContactsBackend trait has two underspecified contracts that a real backend implementor will get wrong (address_book_has_contents bool-not-Result fail-open, JMAP-qz9v.27; and update_object's unspecified patch format, JMAP-qz9v.28), and the README's primary trait-implementation example does not compile (JMAP-qz9v.37, .38). The handler-library surface is otherwise well-shaped — the trait is generic over O for the common operations, the method names match RFC 9610 exactly, and the CallerCtx threading is clean at the signature level. Pre-1.0 status means the immutability findings (.23-.26) can still be cleaned up cheaply; postpone is the wrong default.
API contract confidence: 18% chance of missed issue. Least confident flags: JMAP-qz9v.28 (update_object patch format — depends on the precise RFC 8620 §5.3 reading I did not re-verify against the spec text in this pass; if PatchObject is normatively merge-patch rather than JSON-Pointer the finding is weaker, though the workspace-internal disagreement between handler and reference-impl still stands), JMAP-qz9v.34 (copy_contact_card concrete-vs-generic — the cookie-cutter argument actually points the other way since mail-server has the same shape; the version-coupling concern is theoretical pre-1.0), JMAP-qz9v.31 (realistic-demo-ids no API signal — might be more deliberately scoped than I credited). Uncertainty driven by: (a) did not run any new probes — purely static source review; (b) did not verify RFC 8620 §5.3's exact PatchObject normative wording against the spec text at ~/PROJECT/jmap-chat-spec/references/rfc8620.txt; (c) did not cross-check whether jmap-mail-server has a parallel review epic with identical api-contract findings already filed (possible duplicate work); (d) did not enumerate BackendSetError variants to confirm whether the handler's wildcard arm is correctly defensive vs over-defensive; (e) did not run cargo doc to see whether any of these public items already have #[doc(hidden)] markers I missed in source.
